\description{
Computes a confidence interval for the difference between two
proportions measured on the same units, \eqn{\psi = p_x - p_y}: two
diagnostic procedures applied to the same patients, the proportion of
students answering an item correctly before and after instruction, or
two raters' rates of assigning a category to the same cases. The two
outcomes of a unit are correlated, so the interval for two independent
proportions does not apply. The default is the score interval of Tango
(1998), which inverts the score test of Nam (1997). It stays inside
\eqn{[-1, 1]} and is defined when no pair is discordant. When
discordant pairs are rare, as in the case worked through under
Details, it covers the difference more often than its nominal level,
erring on the conservative side, where the familiar Wald interval
falls well short of that level.
}
\details{
\strong{Only the discordant pairs carry the difference.} Classify the
\eqn{N} units by their pair of outcomes: \eqn{n_{11}} succeed under
both conditions, \eqn{n_{10}} under \code{x} only, \eqn{n_{01}} under
\code{y} only, and \eqn{n_{00}} under neither. The two proportions are
\eqn{(n_{11} + n_{10})/N} and \eqn{(n_{11} + n_{01})/N}, so
\eqn{n_{11}} cancels from their difference,
\eqn{\hat\psi = (n_{10} - n_{01})/N}{psi-hat = (n_10 - n_01)/N}, and
the concordant units enter only through \eqn{N}. That is why the
counts interface asks for \code{n_10}, \code{n_01}, and \code{N} and
nothing more. The variance of the per-unit difference,
\eqn{p_{10} + p_{01} - \psi^2}, depends on
a discordant probability as well as on \eqn{\psi}, so for inference on
\eqn{\psi} the probability \eqn{p_{01}} is a nuisance parameter.

\strong{The score interval.} For a hypothesized difference
\eqn{\psi_0} the score statistic is
\deqn{Z(\psi_0) = \frac{n_{10} - n_{01} - N\psi_0}
  {\sqrt{N\{2\tilde p_{01} + \psi_0(1 - \psi_0)\}}},}{Z(psi_0) =
  (n_10 - n_01 - N psi_0) / sqrt(N [2 p~_01 + psi_0 (1 - psi_0)]),}
where \eqn{\tilde p_{01}}{p~_01} is the maximum likelihood estimate of
\eqn{p_{01}} under the restriction \eqn{p_{10} - p_{01} = \psi_0}, the
larger root of a quadratic (Nam, 1997, equations 1 and 3). The
denominator is \eqn{N} times the variance of the per-unit difference,
evaluated at the restricted estimates rather than at the observed
proportions. With \eqn{\alpha} = 1 - \code{conf_level}, the lower limit
is the value of \eqn{\psi_0} at which \eqn{Z(\psi_0) = z_{1-\alpha/2}}
and the upper limit the value at which \eqn{Z(\psi_0) = -z_{1-\alpha/2}},
found by root finding. At \eqn{\psi_0 = 0} the statistic is McNemar's,
\eqn{(n_{10} - n_{01})/\sqrt{n_{10} + n_{01}}}{(n_10 - n_01) /
sqrt(n_10 + n_01)}, so the score interval excludes 0 exactly when
