\eqn{\alpha}: the interval extends that test to every other value of
the difference. With no discordant pair the limits are
\eqn{\mp z^2/(N + z^2)}{-/+ z^2/(N + z^2)}, with \eqn{z = z_{1-\alpha/2}},
an interval whose width reflects the \eqn{N} units observed rather
than a width of zero.

\strong{The Wald interval.} The interval
\eqn{\hat\psi \pm z_{1-\alpha/2}\{(n_{10} + n_{01})/N -
\hat\psi^2\}^{1/2}/\sqrt{N}}{psi-hat +/- z_(1-alpha/2) sqrt([(n_10 +
n_01)/N - psi-hat^2] / N)}
estimates the variance at the observed proportions instead, and is
available for instruction and comparison. With few discordant pairs it
is too short: it has zero width when no pair is discordant, and it can
extend beyond \eqn{[-1, 1]}. Nam (1997) found by simulation that the
test it inverts rejects more often than its nominal level, while the
score test holds close to the nominal level and tends to the
conservative side. Summing over every possible table with
\eqn{p_{10} = .03}, \eqn{p_{01} = .01}, and \eqn{N = 50}, the 95\%
score interval covers \eqn{\psi} with probability .986 and the 95\%
Wald interval with probability .779.

\strong{Noninferiority and equivalence.} The limits of a
100(1 - 2\eqn{\alpha})\% interval are one-sided
100(1 - \eqn{\alpha})\% confidence bounds, so \code{conf_level = 0.90}
gives the interval that \code{\link{equivalence_proportion_paired}}
